WEBVTT

00:00:03.076 --> 00:00:07.726
The first conversation is about purpose: why does this team exist?

00:00:07.776 --> 00:00:10.726
It sounds abstract, but it’s very practical.

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:17.866
A clear purpose helps a team say no to the wrong work, make decisions faster, and explain to others what they’re for.

00:00:17.916 --> 00:00:25.506
Without it, every request looks equally important, and the team slowly turns into a group of people who happen to share a calendar.

00:00:25.556 --> 00:00:30.166
How do you know your team needs this conversation? Here are four signs.

00:00:30.216 --> 00:00:33.866
People describe the team’s job very differently when you ask them.

00:00:33.916 --> 00:00:36.766
The team says yes to almost everything.

00:00:36.816 --> 00:00:40.666
New people take months to understand what the team is really for.

00:00:40.716 --> 00:00:45.866
Or people talk about tasks and projects, but never about who they’re doing it for.

00:00:45.916 --> 00:00:49.406
The output of this conversation is two things on the canvas.

00:00:49.456 --> 00:00:54.626
First, a purpose statement: “We exist to… for… by…”

00:00:54.676 --> 00:01:01.926
Second, a picture of the future: what will this team have achieved in three years, and how will we notice it’s becoming real?

00:01:01.976 --> 00:01:03.846
One warning before we start.

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:06.186
Most purpose statements are terrible.

00:01:06.236 --> 00:01:12.546
They’re full of words like “excellence”, “value” and “synergies”, and nobody remembers them a week later.

00:01:12.596 --> 00:01:18.306
That’s because they’re written by committee, in organisational language, usually starting from a blank page.

00:01:18.356 --> 00:01:25.483
The exercise in the next lesson avoids that, by starting from how people talk to their friends, not how they talk in strategy documents.

